partialText:
&lt;p&gt;I play fast music, and most metronomes I tried stop at 240 or 300 BPM. So I built one that goes to 1000: &lt;a href="https://unlimitedmetronome.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Unlimited Metronome&lt;/a&gt;. It is free, needs no account and works offline. This post is about the parts that turned out to be harder than "play a click every N milliseconds".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt; is not a metronome
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious version is a timer that plays a sound on every tick. It sounds fine at 80 BPM and falls apart as soon as anything else happens on the page: timers drift, the main thread is busy rendering, and browsers throttle timers in background tabs to once a second or less. At 260 BPM a beat is 230 ms long, so even a 20 ms wobble is audible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is the pattern from Chris Wilson's article "A Tale of Two Clocks": keep two clocks with different jobs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A coarse JavaScript timer wakes up every 25 ms. It never plays anything itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On every wake-up it looks a short window ahead (150 ms) and schedules every click that falls inside that window &lt;strong&gt;on the audio clock&lt;/strong&gt;, with &lt;code&gt;AudioBufferSourceNode.start(time)&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;engine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentTime&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lookahead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;while &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sequencer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;peekTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sequencer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;bpm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulses&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A tick more than 50 ms in the past means the timer stalled:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// skip its sound instead of playing a burst of late clicks.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.05&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;mute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;engine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;scheduleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The audio clock is sample-accurate, so the timer's jitter no longer matters: it only has to wake up before the window runs out. The UI (the beat dots, the bar counter) reads from what has been scheduled and never touches this loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keeping time in a background tab
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Musicians switch to a tab with sheet music or a video and expect the click to keep going. Two things make that work:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The timer runs in a dedicated Web Worker.&lt;/strong&gt; Browsers throttle main-thread timers of hidden tabs hard; a worker is throttled far less. If workers are unavailable it falls back to &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The look-ahead grows while the page is hidden&lt;/strong&gt;, from 150 ms to 1.2 s, so even a late wake-up still finds clicks already queued on the audio clock.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Clicks that do not pile up at 1000 BPM
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At 1000 BPM with sixteenth-note subdivision there are 66 clicks per second, about 15 ms apart. A click sample longer than that overlaps the next one and turns into mush.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The clicks are not samples at all. Each one is synthesised on the device into an &lt;code&gt;AudioBuffer&lt;/code&gt; (a short tone with a few partials, an exponential decay and a little filtered noise in the attack), and every click is rendered no longer than 90% of the time until the next one. The rendered buffers are cached by length in 5 ms buckets, so the cache stays small even when the tempo changes all the time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A speed trainer that changes tempo on the bar line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feature I wanted most was a trainer: start at 220, go up by 1 BPM every 4 bars until 240. The rule that made it feel right is that &lt;strong&gt;tempo changes are only evaluated on bar boundaries&lt;/strong&gt;. The sequencer knows which bar and stage it is in, and a new stage always starts exactly on beat one: no extra click, no missing one, no tempo change in the middle of a bar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the sequencer is plain TypeScript with no audio in it, it is easy to test: give it a plan and a start time, pull ticks, and check that the first tick of every stage lands on a downbeat with the new tempo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Odd meters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Time signatures go from 1 to 32 beats, and beats can be grouped, so 7/8 can be counted as 2+2+3 or 3+2+2. The first beat of each group gets an accent, and any beat can be switched between accent, normal and mute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The rest of the stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React for the UI, with all of the timing outside React.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every page is prerendered to static HTML for each of the 9 languages and served from Cloudflare Pages.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A service worker precaches the app, so after the first visit it works offline and can be installed as a PWA.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No account and no cookies. Settings stay in the browser's local storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you try it, I would love to hear how the timing feels on your device at very high tempos, and what is missing: &lt;a href="https://unlimitedmetronome.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;unlimitedmetronome.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over a decade ago, frontend development was trapped in a cycle of manual DOM tracking and fragmented state. The introduction of React in 2013 shifted that paradigm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before React, most applications leaned on libraries like jQuery. Developers wrote imperative, step-by-step instructions to find an HTML element, update its text, append a child, and manually keep the data layer in sync with the browser. As applications grew, this approach turned codebases into fragile "spaghetti code," where changing a single button could silently break an unrelated form elsewhere on the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Declarative data binding already existed in AngularJS and Knockout, but React reframed the problem. It treated the UI as a function of state: you describe what the screen should look like for the current data, compose it from small components, and let data flow in one direction. React handles the DOM updates under the hood.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compared to the heavyweight MVC-style architecture of AngularJS and early enterprise frameworks, React felt lightweight, predictable, and simple to write. It put layout and logic together in components, captured the imagination of the industry, and its growth became unstoppable. Many of us fell in love with it, spending hundreds of hours on tutorials, docs, and side projects to master it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, React's dominance is unmatched. It has topped the Stack Overflow Developer Survey's web-framework rankings for years and appears in the majority of frontend job listings. The ecosystem is massive, the community resources are enormous, and it is the default choice for engineering teams worldwide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But when you step away from the hype and build complex, production-scale applications, you realize that the promise of "it's just JavaScript" hides a collection of structural trade-offs, architectural taxes, and frustrating quirks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. The Deceptive Ergonomics of React's Rendering Rules
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React's core pitch has always been its closeness to the platform: "If you know JavaScript, you know React." The syntax is fine. The problem is that plain JavaScript semantics leak into a template context, and React adds runtime rules of its own on top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The conditional rendering trap (&lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In JavaScript, the logical AND operator short-circuits: if the left side is falsy, the expression returns that left-hand value. In a template, developers naturally assume a falsy condition means nothing appears on screen.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A natural assumption: if messages is 0, show nothing.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;You have new messages!&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The reality:&lt;/strong&gt; React renders a literal &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; on your page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it happens:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;0 &amp;amp;&amp;amp; &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;…&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; evaluates to the number &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. React skips &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt;, but it treats numbers (including &lt;code&gt;NaN&lt;/code&gt;) as valid content and renders them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This isn't unique to JSX. A plain template literal does the same thing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;innerHTML&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;You have new messages!&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// renders: &amp;lt;div&amp;gt;0&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The real issue is that JSX uses raw JavaScript expressions as its control flow. Template languages with dedicated syntax, like &lt;code&gt;{#if}&lt;/code&gt; in Svelte or &lt;code&gt;v-if&lt;/code&gt; in Vue, evaluate a condition rather than render an expression's value, so the trap doesn't exist. In React, you have to remember to write defensive code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;You have new messages!&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// or&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;You have new messages!&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  The obligatory &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; prop
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In JSX, &lt;code&gt;.map()&lt;/code&gt; is the primary way to render lists:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Natural assumption: map an array straight to the UI&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In development mode, React logs a warning: &lt;em&gt;"Each child in a list should have a unique 'key' prop."&lt;/em&gt; React's reconciler needs a stable identity for each item (&lt;code&gt;key={user.id}&lt;/code&gt;) to work out which elements moved, were added, or were removed between renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To be fair, this is not unique to React. Vue requires &lt;code&gt;:key&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;v-for&lt;/code&gt;, and Svelte has keyed each blocks (&lt;code&gt;{#each users as user (user.id)}&lt;/code&gt;). Any framework that diffs lists needs identity. What makes it a trap in React is that the warning is easy to silence with the array index. If you use &lt;code&gt;key={index}&lt;/code&gt;, then reordering, inserting, or deleting items can cause component state and uncontrolled input values to stay attached to the wrong rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. The Immutability Tax
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Perhaps the most tedious part of daily React development is its reliance on immutability to trigger updates. React decides whether state changed by comparing references with &lt;code&gt;Object.is&lt;/code&gt;, not by inspecting the values inside.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you mutate a nested object directly and pass the same reference back, React bails out:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Plain JavaScript allows direct mutation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;San Francisco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ❌ React will NOT update the screen: same reference as before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To get the UI to update, you must rebuild every object along the path to the changed value:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ⚠️ The React "spreading nightmare"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;San Francisco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This pattern carries real costs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unreadable code.&lt;/strong&gt; Deeply nested updates become a thicket of dots, braces, and spread operators that hide the one line of intent: the city changed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Silent data loss.&lt;/strong&gt; Forget a single &lt;code&gt;...prevUser.profile&lt;/code&gt; and you don't get an error. You quietly wipe out sibling fields.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Allocation churn.&lt;/strong&gt; The spread pattern copies only the objects along the changed path (&lt;code&gt;user → profile → address&lt;/code&gt;); untouched branches are reused by reference. That keeps the cost modest, but in state-heavy UIs that update frequently, such as live dashboards, you still generate a steady stream of short-lived objects for the garbage collector, for work that a mutable model wouldn't need at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ecosystem's standard answer is Immer (&lt;code&gt;useImmer&lt;/code&gt;, or Redux Toolkit, which uses it internally). It lets you write mutation-style code and produces the immutable copy for you. That works, but it's another dependency added to recover the ergonomics plain JavaScript already had.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare that with fine-grained reactive frameworks. In Svelte 5, state declared with &lt;code&gt;$state&lt;/code&gt; is deeply reactive, so the natural mutation simply works:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight svelte"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;$state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Austin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;San Francisco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// UI updates&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onclick=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Move&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/button&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Band-Aids on Band-Aids: Memoization and the React Compiler
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a component's state changes, React re-executes that component function from top to bottom. By default, every child it renders re-executes too, whether or not the child's props changed. (Elements passed in as &lt;code&gt;children&lt;/code&gt; from higher up are the exception, which is why the composition pattern is a common workaround.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To stop the wasted work, React historically pushed developers into a cycle of manual optimization with &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;. Here's what keeping a single delete handler stable looks like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;UserRow&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;memo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;UserRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onDelete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onDelete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;UserList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;deleteUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Stable function identity across renders...&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleDelete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;deleteUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;deleteUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// forget deleteUser here and you call a stale closure&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...and a memoized derived value&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;localeCompare&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;UserRow&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onDelete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleDelete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Note that all three pieces are needed. &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; alone prevents nothing: the child only skips re-rendering because it's wrapped in &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt;. Miss any one of them, or get a dependency array wrong, and the optimization silently stops working. The engine doesn't figure out what needs to update; the cognitive load sits entirely with the developer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The React Compiler
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React team recognized this friction and built the React Compiler, a build-time Babel plugin that reached its stable 1.0 release in 2025 and supports React 17 and later. It analyzes your components and inserts memoization automatically, so you no longer write most &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt; calls by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The compiler closes the ergonomics gap, but it is an automated band-aid on the same underlying model. At runtime, React still re-executes components, compares references, and diffs a Virtual DOM tree to work out which minimal DOM mutations to apply. Fine-grained reactive frameworks like Svelte 5 and Solid skip that step: they track which piece of state each DOM node depends on and update exactly those nodes, with no diffing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's worth noting that Solid uses JSX too. The friction described here comes from React's runtime model, not from JSX syntax itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. The Architectural Divide: Server vs. Client Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern React frameworks have introduced a hard boundary between React Server Components (RSC) and Client Components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In RSC frameworks such as the Next.js App Router, components are Server Components by default and have no interactivity. The moment you need an event handler, local state, or an effect, you must mark the file with a directive at the very top, before any imports:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The rest of your interactive component...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Forget it, and your app errors out. Importing a hook like &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; into a Server Component fails at compile time; passing an &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt; from one fails when it renders. The directive also marks a boundary: everything that file imports becomes client code too, so where you place it shapes your bundle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(In a plain React app built with Vite or similar, none of this applies. Every component is a client component and the directive does nothing.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The divide forces engineers to constantly split layout from interactivity and reason about which side of the network each piece of code runs on. That breaks the simple, unified mental model that made React appealing in the first place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The AI Paradox: More Code Doesn't Equal Better Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've entered an era where developers lean heavily on AI assistants and autonomous agents to write code. That turns the framework debate into a paradox.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LLMs learn from vast amounts of public code, and after a decade of dominance, React is among the most heavily represented frameworks in that code. Models are very good at producing React: component trees, hooks, spread-based state updates, memoization wrappers, all in seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So developers reasonably ask: &lt;em&gt;"If my agent writes the React boilerplate for me, why should I care that it's verbose?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the costs don't disappear; they move:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The token tax.&lt;/strong&gt; Framework authors such as Svelte's Rich Harris have long argued that the same component can be written in substantially less code in a compiled, reactive framework. Every line of boilerplate an agent generates is a line it also has to read back, costing tokens on every pass.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The context limit.&lt;/strong&gt; In AI-driven development, code volume is context weight. The more verbose the codebase, the fewer files fit into an agent's working context, and the less it can reason about your system's broader architecture.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Surface area for bugs.&lt;/strong&gt; Even if an AI writes 1,000 lines of React instantly, your app still executes them, your team still reviews them, and they still carry dependencies that can break in the next major upgrade.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yet the loop continues: we let AI write ever more React, largely because the AI is already good at it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Corporate Gravity: Why Teams Still Choose React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Given these quirks and costs, why do teams still default to React for brand-new projects, passing over modern alternatives that compile components down to direct DOM operations?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reasons are rarely technical. They're rooted in corporate safety, ecosystem lock-in, and professional self-preservation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  "Nobody ever got fired for buying IBM"
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the era of mainframes, IT managers bought IBM because it was the safe, standard choice. If an IBM system failed, nobody blamed the manager; they blamed IBM. If they bought an alternative and it failed, their judgment was on the line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is software engineering's modern IBM. Long backed by Meta, it's now an industry institution in the most literal sense: in October 2025, Meta announced it would move React into an independent React Foundation under the Linux Foundation, with companies such as Vercel, Microsoft, and Amazon among its founding members. If a multi-million-dollar React project misses its deadlines, leadership blames project complexity. If an architect chose a leaner alternative and hit roadblocks, the choice itself would be questioned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The network effect and job security
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React's network effect exerts a gravitational pull. Third-party vendors, payment providers, and component libraries ship their React SDKs first. For many managers, avoiding that integration risk outweighs the architectural benefits of more efficient alternatives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The job market reinforces the cycle. Developers want skills that maximize their options, and React dominates frontend job listings, so engineers push their teams toward it to keep their résumés aligned with hiring trends. It becomes self-fulfilling: companies choose React because developers know it, and developers learn React because companies hire for it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Knowing a Framework Is No Longer the Skill
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI-assisted coding changes what engineering knowledge is worth. Knowing a specific framework's syntax is no longer a premium skill: an AI agent can recall APIs, lifecycle rules, and idioms, and translate code across ecosystems in seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Years of React have already taught us the durable concepts: component state, props, data flow, rendering lifecycles, and web architecture. Moving that knowledge to a leaner framework is easier than it has ever been. The skills that matter now are system design, data orchestration, and clear, simple solutions, not remembering where a &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; belongs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where React Still Wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this means React is the wrong choice everywhere. If you need to share code with a mobile app through React Native, depend on a vendor whose SDK exists only for React, or need to staff a large team quickly, React's ecosystem and hiring pool are real advantages. The point isn't that React is bad. It's that it should be a deliberate choice, not a default.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A Call to Engineering Leaders
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React remains an industry juggernaut, and its position won't change overnight. But choosing it out of habit, fear of change, or institutional momentum holds our codebases back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Engineering leaders should evaluate their stack on its merits, not on comfort or consensus. Prototype the next greenfield project in Svelte 5, Solid, or Vue and measure the result: lines of code, bundle size, rendering performance, and how quickly the team ships. Look at the systemic health of your stack, prioritize developer velocity, and favor architectures that stay close to the web platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React solved the problems of its time, and it solved them brilliantly. But the web has moved on. The friction we've learned to live with, from stray zeros and fragile keys to nested spreads, dependency arrays, compiler band-aids, and &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; boundaries, isn't a sign of a mature tool. It's the cost of a rendering model designed before compiled, fine-grained reactivity was practical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an era where AI can write any framework's syntax, the deciding factors are how much code you ship, how much your team and your agents must read, and how directly your UI maps to your intent. On those measures, React is no longer the obvious winner. It's simply the familiar one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Familiarity is a reason to be careful, not a reason to stop questioning. The best engineering decisions come from measuring, not from following the crowd. It's time to step out from behind the corporate safety net of the Virtual DOM and build a simpler, faster web.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://saravi.hashnode.dev/the-react-illusion-why-the-industry-s-go-to-library-is-full-of-hidden-friction" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;my Hashnode blog&lt;/a&gt;. Follow me there for more posts.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a site with practice exams for Dutch certification exams. Each exam has a few fixed practice forms, at URLs like &lt;code&gt;/examen/b-vca/1/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/examen/b-vca/2/&lt;/code&gt; and so on. Last month I typed &lt;code&gt;/examen/b-vca/999/&lt;/code&gt; to check the error page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page looked right: "Deze pagina bestaat niet" ("this page doesn't exist"), the normal layout, a link back. Then I looked at the status code. &lt;strong&gt;200.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A 404 page served with a 200 is what Google calls a soft 404. Here's why it happens in Next.js 16 with partial prerendering, what I tried, and why I ended up leaving it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the status is already gone
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exam page is built the way Next.js 16 wants you to build it with &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents: true&lt;/code&gt;. The page itself is a static shell, and everything that needs the request goes inside a Suspense boundary:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Oefenexamen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;robots&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;follow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ExamPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PageProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/examen/[exam]/[form]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Notice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Oefenexamen laden…"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExamForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Inside, the component looks up the exam and calls &lt;code&gt;notFound()&lt;/code&gt; when the form number doesn't exist:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exam&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;1-9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;0-9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;*$/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;exam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;forms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fixedCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;notFound&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With partial prerendering, the server sends the prerendered shell first and streams the dynamic part after it. By the time &lt;code&gt;notFound()&lt;/code&gt; runs inside the Suspense boundary, the response headers, including &lt;code&gt;200 OK&lt;/code&gt;, have already gone out. Next.js still renders the not-found UI into the stream, so a person sees the right page. A crawler that only reads the status sees a normal page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;URLs that don't match any route are fine. They go to &lt;code&gt;global-not-found.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and get a real 404. Only an unknown value inside a dynamic route that streams is affected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I tried
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;generateStaticParams&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; List the real forms, and anything else should 404. It doesn't work that way here. The listed params get prerendered, but an unlisted one still gets the shell and renders on demand, so we're back at the same stream.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;dynamicParams = false&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the classic switch for "only the params I listed". With &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/code&gt; enabled, Next.js doesn't allow it on the route.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Making the page fully static.&lt;/strong&gt; Then &lt;code&gt;notFound()&lt;/code&gt; would run at build time and the status would be right. But this page reads the visitor's session cookie and the database (who is this, and do they have access). It can't be static.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A proxy in front of the route.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the one that actually works. A &lt;code&gt;proxy.ts&lt;/code&gt; (what used to be &lt;code&gt;middleware.ts&lt;/code&gt;) checks the exam and form number before the page runs and returns a 404 for unknown ones. The cost is that it runs on &lt;strong&gt;every&lt;/strong&gt; request under &lt;code&gt;/examen/&lt;/code&gt;, including the server actions that save answers and hand in the exam. It also needs a list of valid exam and form pairs at the edge, generated from the content and kept in sync. That's a new moving part on the hottest path in the app, to fix a URL nobody links to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why we kept the soft 404
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What matters about a soft 404 is that a search engine might index an error page or waste crawl budget on it. So I checked what a search engine can actually do with these URLs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every exam page is &lt;code&gt;noindex, nofollow&lt;/code&gt;,&lt;/strong&gt; including the real ones. A practice exam isn't a page anyone should land on from search. The guides that link to it are.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;/examen/&lt;/code&gt; is disallowed in &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; Well-behaved crawlers don't fetch the page at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nothing links to a form that doesn't exist.&lt;/strong&gt; The links to exam forms are generated from the same data that decides which forms exist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So no crawler should ever see the 200, and if one does, the page tells it not to index anything. The fix would add risk to the part of the app where a bug costs a user their exam attempt. The problem it fixes has no visible effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wrote it down as a decision with a condition: &lt;strong&gt;revisit it if a crawler or an uptime monitor ever needs the real status.&lt;/strong&gt; Then the proxy plus a generated exam/form map is the way to do it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd tell someone else on Next.js 16
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check your error status codes with curl, not the browser.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" &amp;lt;url&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; takes a second. The page looking right tells you nothing about the header.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;notFound()&lt;/code&gt; inside Suspense can't change the status&lt;/strong&gt; once the shell has streamed. If the status matters, the check has to happen before streaming starts: at build time, or in a proxy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Decide per route whether the status matters.&lt;/strong&gt; On public, indexable routes it does, so make those static or check them early. On routes that are &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; and disallowed anyway, a soft 404 can be the cheaper, safer trade.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Write the decision down with the condition that would change it.&lt;/strong&gt; Otherwise someone (probably you) rediscovers the 200 in three months and spends an evening on it again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site is &lt;a href="https://examenzaal.nl/examens/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;examenzaal.nl&lt;/a&gt;, practice exams for Dutch certificates. The real exam forms start from pages like the &lt;a href="https://examenzaal.nl/vca/b-vca/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;B-VCA practice page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I wrote this post with help from an AI assistant. The code and the decision come from our own repository.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built with react js, Tailwind, lenis, and GSAP.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live site : &lt;a href="https://ns-trip.heysimplice.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://ns-trip.heysimplice.com/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Source code : &lt;a href="https://github.com/njoh-simplice/pagecase-pages%EF%BF%BC" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/njoh-simplice/pagecase-pages￼&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Free to fork. Make it yours&lt;br&gt;
What would you improve ?￼&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PurpleWallet started as a backend exercise:&lt;/strong&gt; a Node.js wallet API with Prisma, Redis and a few queues. Over the past few months it grew into a complete product, with a React web app, Paystack payments, a double-entry ledger, an admin console, identity checks, AI spending insights and two layers of automated tests. It's live, and it costs nothing to run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are the parts I found most interesting to build, and the surprises I hit putting it online.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://walletpurple.netlify.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;walletpurple.netlify.app&lt;/a&gt;. Click &lt;strong&gt;"Use the demo account"&lt;/strong&gt; on the login page, or sign up for your own. Payments run in Paystack's test mode, so no real money moves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Code:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/tochi27/Wallet_System" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/tochi27/Wallet_System&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Layer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Choice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node.js, Express 5, TypeScript, Zod&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PostgreSQL with Prisma; Redis with BullMQ&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Web app&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React 19, Vite, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, TanStack Query&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Payments and AI&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Paystack (test mode), Google Gemini&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Jest and Supertest, Playwright&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both apps live in one npm workspaces monorepo, so a single pull request can change the API, the web app and the browser tests together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Moving money, exactly once
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tempting shortcut is to credit the wallet when the user lands back from Paystack's checkout. That's wrong twice over: the redirect can be faked, and the same payment gets reported several times, by the redirect, by Paystack's webhook, and by retries of both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So PurpleWallet never trusts the redirect. Whichever signal arrives first triggers a server-side check with Paystack, and the deposit is credited inside a database transaction that first &lt;strong&gt;claims&lt;/strong&gt; it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Only one caller can move the deposit out of PENDING; everyone else gets count 0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;claimed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;deposit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateMany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;deposit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DepositStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PENDING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DepositStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SUCCESSFUL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;channel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;channel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;paidAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;claimed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// already credited by the webhook, the redirect or a retry&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The webhook, the redirect and a retry can all race, and the wallet is still credited once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Withdrawals work the other way round: the wallet is debited &lt;strong&gt;first&lt;/strong&gt;, so the money can't be spent twice while the bank transfer is in flight. A failed transfer is refunded exactly once with the same claim pattern, and a job every five minutes picks up anything a webhook missed. On top of that, an &lt;code&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/code&gt; header and a Redis lock per wallet stop a double-tapped "Send" button from sending twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Books that always balance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;code&gt;balance&lt;/code&gt; column works until a number looks wrong and you have nothing to check it against. So PurpleWallet also keeps a &lt;strong&gt;double-entry ledger&lt;/strong&gt;. Every movement of money is a journal entry whose lines sum to zero. A ₦5,000 transfer from Ada to Bola:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Account&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Amount&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Wallet: Ada&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+5,000.00 (debit: we owe Ada less)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Wallet: Bola&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;−5,000.00 (credit: we owe Bola more)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Total&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0.00&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The database enforces the rules, not just the code. A &lt;strong&gt;deferred constraint trigger&lt;/strong&gt; rejects any transaction that leaves an entry unbalanced, and &lt;strong&gt;append-only triggers&lt;/strong&gt; block edits and deletes, so mistakes are fixed with reversing entries, as an accountant would.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The admin console then runs a &lt;strong&gt;three-way reconciliation&lt;/strong&gt;: for every wallet, it compares the stored balance, the sum of its transactions and the ledger. The trial balance should always read ₦0.00. It does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Security that behaves like a bank's
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Transaction PIN.&lt;/strong&gt; Transfers and withdrawals need a 4-digit PIN. Five wrong attempts lock money movement for 30 minutes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verified email before money moves.&lt;/strong&gt; Unverified accounts can look around but get &lt;code&gt;403&lt;/code&gt; on anything that moves money.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Careful password resets.&lt;/strong&gt; Links are single-use, expire in 30 minutes, and are stored only as a hash. Changing a password signs out every other session.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Never &lt;code&gt;401&lt;/code&gt; for a wrong PIN.&lt;/strong&gt; The web app treats &lt;code&gt;401&lt;/code&gt; as "session expired", and a typo must never sign someone out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Limits and tiers.&lt;/strong&gt; Tier 1 allows ₦50,000 per payment, ₦200,000 a day and a ₦500,000 balance. The checks run inside the payment's own transaction and lock, so two simultaneous payments can't both slip under the limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Identity checks, handled like sensitive data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To reach Tier 2 (₦500,000 per payment, ₦2,000,000 a day, no balance cap), users upload a photo of their ID and a selfie holding it, and staff approve or reject the request with a reason.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ID photos are the most sensitive data the app holds, so they're checked by their &lt;strong&gt;contents&lt;/strong&gt;, not their file names, then &lt;strong&gt;re-encoded&lt;/strong&gt; to strip GPS and camera details, and stored &lt;strong&gt;AES-256-GCM encrypted&lt;/strong&gt; with a key that never touches the database. Only staff with a dedicated permission can open them, every opening is audit-logged, and photos from rejected requests are deleted after 30 days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. An admin console with checks and balances
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Staff have four roles: &lt;strong&gt;Viewer&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Support&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Finance&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Owner&lt;/strong&gt;. Balance adjustments over ₦10,000 use &lt;strong&gt;maker-checker&lt;/strong&gt;: a second admin must approve, and both steps require re-entering a password. Every admin action lands in an append-only audit log.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. AI that can't make up numbers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PurpleWallet uses Google Gemini for monthly &lt;strong&gt;spending insights&lt;/strong&gt; and an &lt;strong&gt;assistant&lt;/strong&gt; you can ask about your money.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule: &lt;strong&gt;the AI never does arithmetic.&lt;/strong&gt; My code computes every figure first, and Gemini only writes the sentences. Every ₦ amount and percentage in its text is then checked against the computed figures. If the model quotes a number the code didn't produce, the text is rejected and the app falls back to template sentences. Names are swapped for aliases like "Person A" before anything leaves the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The assistant uses &lt;strong&gt;read-only tools&lt;/strong&gt;. It can't move money: when it suggests a transfer, it pre-fills the Send dialog, and the user still confirms with their PIN.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. Tests at two levels
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The API has &lt;strong&gt;562 Jest tests&lt;/strong&gt;, including the nasty cases: two deposits racing, a refund claimed twice, a limit hit by simultaneous payments. &lt;strong&gt;Playwright tests&lt;/strong&gt; then drive the real app in Chrome against a throwaway database, covering sign-up, sending money, wrong PINs, limits, ID checks and admin approvals. GitHub Actions runs both on every pull request, and again before each deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CI had its own bug. Change detection worked on pull requests, but on merge commits it decided nothing had changed, so after every merge the tests &lt;strong&gt;and the deploy&lt;/strong&gt; were skipped, while the run still showed a green tick. Pushes to &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; now always test and deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  8. Hosting for ₦0, and what the free tiers taught me
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The web app runs on Netlify, the API on Render, Postgres on Supabase and Redis on Redis Cloud, all on free plans. Each had a surprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A "free" deploy that wasn't.&lt;/strong&gt; Because my Render Blueprint didn't say &lt;code&gt;plan: free&lt;/code&gt;, Render used its paid default, and months later suspended the account over an unpaid invoice. Render kindly waived it, and the Blueprint now states the plan explicitly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Free plans block outgoing email.&lt;/strong&gt; Render's free tier blocks SMTP, so verification emails silently timed out. The fix was a short Google Apps Script deployed in the sending Gmail account: the API posts each email to it over HTTPS with a shared secret, and the script sends it through Gmail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Supabase exposes your tables by default.&lt;/strong&gt; Supabase emailed a critical warning: every table was reachable through its auto-generated Data API, because row-level security was off. My app never uses that API, but the door was open. A migration now enables row-level security and revokes the API roles' access, and a test fails CI if a new migration forgets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Free services sleep.&lt;/strong&gt; A scheduled ping every 10 minutes keeps the API awake, using about 730 of Render's 750 free hours a month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  9. A demo you can share safely
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The live site has a &lt;strong&gt;one-click demo account&lt;/strong&gt; with three months of realistic Nigerian spending (rent, Shoprite, Bolt rides, DSTV), so the insights and assistant have something to talk about. Since everyone shares that login, visitors can't change its password or PIN, wrong PINs never lock it, and it never receives email. There's also a sample ID card to download for trying the identity check, so nobody uploads a real one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd tell myself at the start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Make the database enforce the rules that matter.&lt;/strong&gt; Triggers caught mistakes that tests alone would have missed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Design for duplicates.&lt;/strong&gt; Webhooks, retries and double-clicks will deliver the same event twice; a conditional "claim" update solves most of it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Let code do the maths and AI do the words&lt;/strong&gt;, then check the words.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Read the free-tier small print&lt;/strong&gt;, and remember that a green CI tick only means something if the jobs actually ran.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PurpleWallet is live at &lt;a href="https://walletpurple.netlify.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;walletpurple.netlify.app&lt;/a&gt;, and the code is on &lt;a href="https://github.com/tochi27/Wallet_System" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. Try the demo, send some test money, or ask the assistant where your money went last month. I'd love to hear what you think.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agents love “fixing” forms. You ask for one rule — zip required before city on blur — and the PR rewrites the whole validation layer, swaps the library, and invents three new error keys nobody asked for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve started forcing &lt;strong&gt;patch-sized&lt;/strong&gt; validation edits. One rule, one file when possible, same patterns as the neighboring fields. Smaller diffs review faster and break less.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What “patch-sized” means here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One rule per prompt&lt;/strong&gt; (or one tightly related pair, like “required + format for email”)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Touch the &lt;strong&gt;existing&lt;/strong&gt; validator / schema / &lt;code&gt;onBlur&lt;/code&gt; helper — don’t introduce a second system&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reuse existing error copy keys and i18n paths&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No new deps unless the ticket already approved them&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Leave happy-path UI alone unless the rule literally needs a new affordance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the agent can’t do that without a rewrite, the rewrite is a separate PR with its own review budget.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prompt shape that keeps diffs small
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Edit US zip-before-city validation in &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`shippingValidation.ts`&lt;/span&gt; only.

Constraints:
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; Add one rule: on blur of city, require zip first (existing error key &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`errors.zipRequired`&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; Do not change Checkout, Billing, or the shared schema package
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; Do not add zod / yup / new deps
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; Match the pattern used by &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`phoneRequired`&lt;/span&gt; in the same file
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; Show me the unified diff before writing other files

If you need more than ~40 lines or a second file, stop and explain why.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The last line matters. Agents expand scope quietly; a hard line-count stop makes them ask instead of “cleaning up” the file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I reject in review
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reject / send back&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;New validation library for one rule&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Renamed every error key “for consistency”&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Form component rewrite when only the rule changed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shared schema change that ripples into Checkout with no ticket&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Accept&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Small diff in the existing validator&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Same error key style as neighbors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tests that cover the new rule (and only that rule) if the file already has them&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why this beats “just let the agent finish”
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A full rewrite can look cleaner. It also:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hides the one-line bug you actually wanted fixed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mixes intentional rule changes with drive-by refactors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Makes bisect and revert painful&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Patch-sized keeps the merge about the product rule. Refactors wait for a PR titled like a refactor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Tiny habit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next time you ask an agent to tweak form validation, paste the constraints above and demand a unified diff first. If the draft already spans three files, stop — narrow the prompt and try again. The rule you wanted usually fits in one patch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You split the project in two: Laravel serves an API on &lt;code&gt;localhost:8000&lt;/code&gt;, and a React or Vue app runs on &lt;code&gt;localhost:5173&lt;/code&gt;. The first request from the browser fails, and the console shows a message along these lines (Chrome's wording):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Access to fetch at 'http://localhost:8000/api/users' from origin 'http://localhost:5173'
has been blocked by CORS policy: No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is present
on the requested resource.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The API itself is usually fine. CORS is a browser rule: the browser asks the server whether a page from another origin may read its response, and the server answers with headers. Laravel already has the machinery for that answer. Most fixes are a few lines of config, and I checked each one against the real middleware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why you only see it in the browser
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two URLs have the same origin only if scheme, host and port all match. &lt;code&gt;localhost:5173&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;localhost:8000&lt;/code&gt; differ in the port, so every request between them is cross-origin. The server never blocks anything. It sends &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-*&lt;/code&gt; headers, and the browser decides whether your JavaScript may see the response. That is why &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt; and Postman work while the browser fails, and why the real fix always means changing what the server sends, not changing the frontend code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Requests that use &lt;code&gt;Authorization&lt;/code&gt;, JSON content types or methods like &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;DELETE&lt;/code&gt; trigger a &lt;strong&gt;preflight&lt;/strong&gt; first: the browser sends an &lt;code&gt;OPTIONS&lt;/code&gt; request asking permission, and only sends the real request if the answer allows it. A CORS problem is very often a failed preflight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How Laravel handles CORS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Laravel's &lt;code&gt;HandleCors&lt;/code&gt; middleware is in the global middleware stack by default, and it uses the &lt;code&gt;fruitcake/php-cors&lt;/code&gt; library. In Laravel 11 and newer, a fresh application does not have a &lt;code&gt;config/cors.php&lt;/code&gt; file, but the framework supplies one. In the Laravel 13 source (13.38.0) the defaults are:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight php"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'paths'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'api/*'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sanctum/csrf-cookie'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_methods'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'*'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_origins'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'*'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_origins_patterns'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_headers'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'*'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'exposed_headers'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'max_age'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'supports_credentials'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To change anything, publish the file and edit it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;php artisan config:publish cors
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Also note that a new Laravel 11+ skeleton has no &lt;code&gt;routes/api.php&lt;/code&gt; until you run &lt;code&gt;php artisan install:api&lt;/code&gt;. If your frontend calls &lt;code&gt;/api/...&lt;/code&gt; and gets a 404, that is a routing problem, not CORS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the preflight without a browser
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reproduce what the browser sends and read the headers that come back:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; OPTIONS http://localhost:8000/api/users &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Origin: http://localhost:5173'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Access-Control-Request-Method: POST'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type,authorization'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A healthy answer is a 204 with &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Methods&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Headers&lt;/code&gt;. I ran this through the real &lt;code&gt;HandleCors&lt;/code&gt; middleware and &lt;code&gt;fruitcake/php-cors&lt;/code&gt; with the default config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;status 204
access-control-allow-origin: *
access-control-allow-methods: POST
access-control-allow-headers: content-type,authorization
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With defaults, a plain API on &lt;code&gt;/api/*&lt;/code&gt; using bearer tokens works from any origin. If yours does not, the cause is one of the cases below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cause 1: The path is not in &lt;code&gt;paths&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CORS headers are only added for URLs that match &lt;code&gt;paths&lt;/code&gt;. A request to &lt;code&gt;/login&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/broadcasting/auth&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/graphql&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;/storage/...&lt;/code&gt; gets none, because those do not start with &lt;code&gt;api/&lt;/code&gt;. In my test, a preflight to &lt;code&gt;/login&lt;/code&gt; with the default config came back with no CORS headers at all. Add what you serve to the frontend:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight php"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'paths'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'api/*'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sanctum/csrf-cookie'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'login'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'logout'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'broadcasting/auth'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Patterns use the same matching as &lt;code&gt;$request-&amp;gt;is()&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; is a wildcard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cause 2: Cookies or sessions (Sanctum SPA) need credentials
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your React or Vue app logs in through cookies, the browser will not send or accept them across origins unless both sides opt in. On the server:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight php"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_origins'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'http://localhost:5173'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// an explicit list, not '*'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'supports_credentials'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In my test, a preflight with that configuration returned:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;access-control-allow-origin: http://localhost:5173
access-control-allow-credentials: true
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On the frontend, the request must ask to include cookies. With axios that is &lt;code&gt;withCredentials: true&lt;/code&gt;, with fetch it is &lt;code&gt;credentials: 'include'&lt;/code&gt;. The Sanctum SPA setup needs more besides CORS: the stateful-domains setting, the session cookie domain, and a call to &lt;code&gt;/sanctum/csrf-cookie&lt;/code&gt; before logging in. Check the Sanctum documentation for those, since I only tested the CORS part. In Laravel 13, &lt;code&gt;$middleware-&amp;gt;statefulApi()&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;bootstrap/app.php&lt;/code&gt; turns on Sanctum's frontend middleware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Do not combine credentials with &lt;code&gt;'*'&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browsers refuse a wildcard origin on a credentialed request. What surprised me is what Laravel does with that configuration. With &lt;code&gt;allowed_origins =&amp;gt; ['*']&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;supports_credentials =&amp;gt; true&lt;/code&gt;, a request from &lt;code&gt;http://localhost:5173&lt;/code&gt; got this back:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;access-control-allow-origin: http://localhost:5173
vary: Origin
access-control-allow-credentials: true
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The library reflected the caller's origin instead of sending &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt;. That makes it work in the browser, and it also means any website can make credentialed requests to your API and read the answers. For production, list your real frontend origins (or use &lt;code&gt;allowed_origins_patterns&lt;/code&gt; with a strict regex) and never leave &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; on when &lt;code&gt;supports_credentials&lt;/code&gt; is true.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cause 3: A request header is not allowed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The default &lt;code&gt;allowed_headers&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;['*']&lt;/code&gt;. If you set an explicit list and forget &lt;code&gt;Authorization&lt;/code&gt; or a custom header, the preflight succeeds but the browser then blocks the real request. In my test, with &lt;code&gt;['Content-Type', 'X-Requested-With']&lt;/code&gt; and a preflight asking for &lt;code&gt;authorization&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;access-control-allow-headers: content-type, x-requested-with
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is no &lt;code&gt;authorization&lt;/code&gt; in the list, so the browser refuses to send it. Add the header to the list, or go back to &lt;code&gt;['*']&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cause 4: The origin is not listed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you set &lt;code&gt;allowed_origins&lt;/code&gt; to one origin, the response carries that origin whatever the caller sends. With &lt;code&gt;['http://localhost:5173']&lt;/code&gt;, a request from &lt;code&gt;http://evil.test&lt;/code&gt; still got &lt;code&gt;access-control-allow-origin: http://localhost:5173&lt;/code&gt;, and the browser rejected it because it does not match the page. When the browser says the origin is not allowed, compare it character by character: &lt;code&gt;http&lt;/code&gt; against &lt;code&gt;https&lt;/code&gt;, a trailing slash, &lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt; against &lt;code&gt;localhost&lt;/code&gt;, and the port are all different origins. For several environments, use a list or a pattern:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight php"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_origins'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'FRONTEND_URL'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'http://localhost:5173'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'allowed_origins_patterns'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'#^https://.*\.example\.com$#'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Things that make it look like a CORS error
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stale config.&lt;/strong&gt; After editing &lt;code&gt;config/cors.php&lt;/code&gt; on a server that caches config, run &lt;code&gt;php artisan config:clear&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;config:cache&lt;/code&gt; again). The cached copy wins over the file.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The real error is something else.&lt;/strong&gt; Open the browser's Network tab and look at the status code of the request. A 404, 419 or 401 from Laravel is not a CORS problem. In my test, a 500 response from the application still carried the CORS header, so you should see the real status. Errors produced by the web server or a proxy in front of Laravel (502, 504, 413) do not pass through the middleware and arrive without CORS headers, so the browser reports them as CORS failures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Headers added twice.&lt;/strong&gt; If Nginx or Apache also adds &lt;code&gt;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&lt;/code&gt;, the browser can receive two values and reject the response. Set CORS in one place only. I did not test this.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Redirects.&lt;/strong&gt; A request that Laravel redirects (for example an unauthenticated browser request sent to a login page) can fail the CORS check on the redirect. Send &lt;code&gt;Accept: application/json&lt;/code&gt; from your API client so Laravel answers with JSON errors instead of redirects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dev proxy as an alternative.&lt;/strong&gt; Vite can proxy &lt;code&gt;/api&lt;/code&gt; to Laravel during development, which removes CORS from local development. That hides a misconfiguration you will meet in production, so keep the real settings correct as well.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://laravel.com/docs/routing#cors" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Laravel Documentation: Routing, CORS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/fruitcake/php-cors" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;fruitcake/php-cors on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://laravel.com/docs/sanctum#spa-authentication" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Laravel Documentation: Sanctum, SPA authentication&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://dev-talk.com/post/laravel-cors-error-react-vue-frontend" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DEV Talk&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Processing PDFs entirely on the client side is becoming the gold standard for modern web applications. It provides zero-latency interactions, eliminates expensive backend compute (no headless Chrome or Ghostscript clusters), and guarantees 100% data privacy since documents never leave the user's browser sandbox.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, once you move from toy examples to handling real-world 50MB+ PDFs, you inevitably encounter three major bottlenecks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Silent Memory Leaks&lt;/strong&gt;: Generating visual thumbnails in &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;canvas&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; easily spikes tab memory past 1.5GB, crashing mobile and low-spec machines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Text Degradation&lt;/strong&gt;: Many naive implementations convert pages into raster images, causing selectable vector glyphs to turn into blurry bitmaps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict Binary Parser Failures&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; is brilliant for lossless stream operations, but it strictly enforces PDF standards. When a user uploads a slightly malformed or non-compliant document, it throws &lt;code&gt;Error: Expected instance of PDFDict&lt;/code&gt;, breaking the workflow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this deep dive, I'll walk through the architectural patterns and production-ready code we used to build a robust, dual-engine PDF pipeline using &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;pdfjs-dist&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Dynamic Singleton PDF.js Worker in Modern ESM Frameworks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In modern SSR/SSG environments (Astro, Next.js, Vite), bundling &lt;code&gt;pdfjs-dist&lt;/code&gt; can cause build errors due to Node/browser mismatch and worker asset loading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of bundling worker code into your main bundle, load it dynamically and maintain a singleton promise:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFJS_CMAP_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/cmaps/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFJS_STANDARD_FONTS_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/standard_fonts/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjsPromise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getPdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjsPromise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjsPromise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pdfjs-dist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Point to static worker copied in your public/ directory&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;GlobalWorkerOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;workerSrc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/pdf.worker.min.mjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;})();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjsPromise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Rendering Page Thumbnails Without the V8 Memory Leak
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When rendering thumbnails for multi-page documents (e.g., in a visual reordering grid), many developers make the mistake of leaving canvas backing stores alive in memory or creating hundreds of uncollected &lt;code&gt;Blob&lt;/code&gt; objects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the exact pattern to calculate proportional scaling, fill white backgrounds for transparent documents, and &lt;strong&gt;immediately release the backing store&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ThumbnailResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;dataUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;aspectRatio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;pageCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderPageThumbnail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1-indexed&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetWidth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;320&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ThumbnailResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;aspectRatio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Calculate dynamic scale so thumbnail width matches targetWidth&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetWidth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;alpha&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Canvas 2D context not available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Render solid white background for transparent PDFs&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fillStyle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#ffffff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fillRect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;canvasContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dataUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toDataURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;image/jpeg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.85&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ⚠️ CRITICAL: Tear down canvas dimensions immediately&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// This signals the browser engine to deallocate GPU/CPU backing store memory&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dataUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;aspectRatio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Lossless Page Reordering &amp;amp; Rotation via Direct Stream Clones
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To preserve vector glyphs, hyperlinks, and document fidelity, you must manipulate the document dictionary directly rather than rasterizing pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a user reorganizes pages (e.g., via drag-and-drop) or applies rotations, calculate the delta on top of the document's original rotation header:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;degrees&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PageItem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;originalIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;rotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// User rotation offset: 0, 90, 180, 270&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;isDeleted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;exportOrganizedPdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;File&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;activePages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PageItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ignoreEncryption allows reading non-restricted structural dictionaries&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sourceDoc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ignoreEncryption&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newDoc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;activePages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;activePages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Copy the specific page dictionary by original index&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;copiedPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;copyPages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sourceDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;originalIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Merge existing base rotation with user rotation&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseRotation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;copiedPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;angle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;finalRotation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseRotation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;360&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;copiedPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;degrees&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;finalRotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;copiedPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfBytes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;save&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;application/pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


  4. The Secret Weapon: Dual-Engine Auto-Healing for Malformed PDFs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the real-world headache: &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; is strict. If an uploaded PDF has a damaged dictionary or corrupt cross-reference table, &lt;code&gt;PDFDocument.load()&lt;/code&gt; throws:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Expected instance of PDFDict, but got instance of ...&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, PDF.js (the battle-tested renderer powering Firefox and Chrome's PDF viewer) has decades of heuristic error-recovery built in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; fails, our pipeline intercepts the error and gracefully falls back to &lt;strong&gt;PDF.js Raster Healing&lt;/strong&gt;: it parses the corrupt file through PDF.js, renders high-fidelity 2.0x (~150 DPI) raster planes, and re-embeds them into a pristine &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; document:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;recoverAndEmbedMalformedPdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mergedDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Target PDFDocument instance from pdf-lib&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;File&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rotationOffset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getPdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;PDF recovery engine unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadingTask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;cMapUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFJS_CMAP_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;cMapPacked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;standardFontDataUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PDFJS_STANDARD_FONTS_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfDoc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadingTask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;numPages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;numPages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;numPages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pdfDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageWidthPt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageHeightPt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unscaledViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Render at 2.0x scale (~144-150 DPI) for crisp, readable text and lines&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderScale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;2.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getViewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderScale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;alpha&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Canvas 2D context unavailable during PDF recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fillStyle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#ffffff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fillRect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;canvasContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;viewport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Blob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBlob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;image/jpeg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.92&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Free canvas GPU/CPU memory immediately&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Failed to rasterize recovery page &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;imgBuffer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;arrayBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;embeddedImg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mergedDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;embedJpg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;imgBuffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Reconstruct a pristine page matching original dimensions&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newPage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mergedDoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageWidthPt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageHeightPt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;drawImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;embeddedImg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageWidthPt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageHeightPt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rotationOffset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;degrees&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;degrees&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rotationOffset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;numPages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


  Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Don't choose between &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;pdf.js&lt;/code&gt;—use both&lt;/strong&gt;:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/code&gt; for fast, vector-lossless operations on valid PDFs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;pdf.js&lt;/code&gt; for UI thumbnails and resilient fallback recovery for corrupt files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Always zero out &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;canvas&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; dimensions&lt;/strong&gt;:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Calling &lt;code&gt;canvas.width = 0; canvas.height = 0;&lt;/code&gt; triggers immediate garbage collection of the underlying GPU bitmap context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Respect base rotation&lt;/strong&gt;:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Never assume incoming PDF pages are at &lt;code&gt;0°&lt;/code&gt;. Always read &lt;code&gt;page.getRotation().angle&lt;/code&gt; before applying user offsets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Interactive Demo &amp;amp; Benchmarking
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to test this dual-engine pipeline with your own complex or malformed PDFs (and inspect DevTools to verify that zero bytes ever leave your browser), feel free to check out our live implementation at &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://versapdf.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VersaPDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How are you currently handling PDF binary operations on the client side? Let me know in the comments if you've encountered other edge cases with non-standard PDFs!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://dev.to/otiswanglei/why-i-built-standard-search-paramsreact-around-standard-schema-39lc"&gt;my previous post&lt;/a&gt; I introduced &lt;code&gt;@standard-search-params/react&lt;/code&gt;, a React hook that validates URL search params against any Standard Schema library. This post is about three decisions I changed my mind on while building v0.2.0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It isn't a story about a clean design arriving fully formed. It's a record of where my first idea was wrong and why I changed it, in case it's useful to anyone else building a small library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A note on process: I used AI (Claude and Claude Code) for design discussion and code review. Everything below is something I could confirm in the implementation and the tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Background: how the hook behaves
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hook reads the URL search params &lt;strong&gt;once, on mount&lt;/strong&gt;, and validates each one against its own schema.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;coerce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;validatedSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useStandardSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;v0.2.0 added a &lt;code&gt;listenToPopstate&lt;/code&gt; option and a &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; function for re-reading the URL after it changes. Building those two things is what led to the three reversals below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. "Wrap your schema in useMemo" fixed nothing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Context
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the set of schema keys changes at runtime, for example when only admin users should have an extra param validated. Because the hook only reads the URL on mount, keys added later are &lt;strong&gt;silently ignored&lt;/strong&gt;. I decided to add a development warning so the problem would be noticed early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  My first idea had two holes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first idea was to warn whenever the schema's &lt;em&gt;reference&lt;/em&gt; changed, and to tell people in the docs to "memoize your schema with &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hole 1: it would fire constantly.&lt;/strong&gt; The README says it's fine to write the schema inline in the component. An inline schema gets a new reference on every render, so a reference check would warn on the very pattern I recommend. A warning that fires every time is a warning nobody reads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I compare the &lt;strong&gt;set of keys&lt;/strong&gt; instead of the reference:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;schemaKeys&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevSchemaKeysRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schemaKeys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isDev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevSchemaKeysRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;schemaKeys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;warn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* ... */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prevSchemaKeysRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;schemaKeys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schemaKeys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now an inline schema (same keys, new reference) stays quiet, and only a real change in the key set warns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hole 2: &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; doesn't fix anything.&lt;/strong&gt; This was the more important one. The hook's initial read runs in an effect on mount, once. Stabilizing the schema's reference with &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; never triggers a re-parse. &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; only guarantees that the reference stays the same. It has no effect like "re-read when the contents change".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So "use &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;" was advice that looked like a fix and fixed nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct fix is to &lt;strong&gt;remount&lt;/strong&gt; the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SearchResults&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isAdmin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;admin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The warning message says so:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;useStandardSearchParams: schema keys changed after mount (was [page], now [page,q]),
but this hook only reads the URL once, on mount, so the new keys won't be read.
If the set of keys genuinely needs to change at runtime, remount the component
(e.g. with a `key` prop) — memoizing the schema object does not cause a re-parse.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Two implementation mistakes too
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A Rules of Hooks violation.&lt;/strong&gt; My first version called the hooks inside a condition: &lt;code&gt;if (isDev) { useRef(...); useEffect(...) }&lt;/code&gt;. Even if the value is fixed at build time, writing it that way fails &lt;code&gt;react-hooks/rules-of-hooks&lt;/code&gt;. The hooks are called unconditionally, and the &lt;code&gt;isDev&lt;/code&gt; check lives inside the effect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Checking that &lt;code&gt;process&lt;/code&gt; exists.&lt;/strong&gt; A bare &lt;code&gt;process.env.NODE_ENV&lt;/code&gt; throws a &lt;code&gt;ReferenceError&lt;/code&gt; in environments without a bundler. Check &lt;code&gt;typeof process !== 'undefined'&lt;/code&gt; first.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isDev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;NODE_ENV&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;production&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  What I took from it
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before writing a fix into a warning or a doc, check that it actually works in the implementation. Here that was a matter of looking at an effect's dependency array.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This warning also has a limit. If the keys stay the same but a validator's contents change (say &lt;code&gt;max(5)&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;max(10)&lt;/code&gt;), it can't tell. Standard Schema's internals differ between libraries, so comparing contents in a general way isn't realistic, and I decided to accept that gap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. I didn't turn popstate on by default
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; event fires when the user presses the browser's back or forward button. My first plan was to listen for it by default: it's a native API, the listener is cheap, and the side effects seemed small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What changed my mind was consistency with the library's own design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The core of this hook is predictable behavior: the URL is read &lt;strong&gt;once, on mount&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The README, the docstrings, and the warning above are all written on that premise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Turning &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; on by default means "values change by themselves when you press back" would apply to existing users too. The API doesn't break, but &lt;strong&gt;the behavior changes without telling anyone&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So it's off by default, and you opt in explicitly:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;validatedSearchParams&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useStandardSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;listenToPopstate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One more detail. SPA router navigation such as &lt;code&gt;router.push()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;navigate()&lt;/code&gt; only pushes a history entry, and it doesn't fire &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt;. So &lt;code&gt;listenToPopstate&lt;/code&gt; doesn't cover that case either. For that, call &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; whenever the router's location changes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Next.js example&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;validatedSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useStandardSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;usePathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSearchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  3. &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; deduplication created a "silently does nothing" case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Context
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a back navigation, both the &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; listener and a &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; call from the router's effect can fire for the same navigation. Parsing twice is wasteful, so I made &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; return early when &lt;code&gt;location.search&lt;/code&gt; is the same as the last one it read.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The problem
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That deduplication had a side effect I missed. If &lt;strong&gt;the URL is the same but the schema's rules have changed&lt;/strong&gt; and you want to re-validate, &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; is silently ignored.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The name &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; suggests "read again when I call it." Silently doing nothing because of an internal optimization doesn't match what the name promises.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The options
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(a) Document that it only has an effect when the URL changes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(b) Add a flag to force it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I chose (b). A mismatch between a name and its behavior should be fixed in the design, not papered over in the docs. The deduplication exists to serve the automatic &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; listener. An explicit call to &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; is entitled to an escape hatch.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;refreshOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;force&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lastSearchRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentSearch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;force&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// re-validate against the current schema even if the URL is the same&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The default stays "no force." If you call &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; on every router navigation, many navigations leave the query string unchanged, and re-validating each time would be wasteful. Only the unusual case, where you want to re-read the same URL, requires you to opt in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tests pin this behavior down: with the same URL and a changed schema, no &lt;code&gt;force&lt;/code&gt; is ignored and &lt;code&gt;force: true&lt;/code&gt; takes effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Two smaller fixes in the implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; on the return value.&lt;/strong&gt; In my design draft, the return value had turned back into a plain object literal. That returns a new reference on every render and breaks the existing test that checks referential stability. I went back to wrapping it in &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Merging state into one &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; Keeping &lt;code&gt;isSearchParamsReady&lt;/code&gt; in a separate &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; means two &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; calls per &lt;code&gt;parse()&lt;/code&gt;. React 18 batches them automatically, but this library's &lt;code&gt;peerDependencies&lt;/code&gt; allow &lt;code&gt;react &amp;gt;=16.8&lt;/code&gt;. In React 17 and earlier, a &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; called from a native event like &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; isn't batched, so it can cause two re-renders. Putting the values in a single state object makes it one &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Wrapping up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These three changes share a way of thinking:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Don't silently change behavior, and don't silently do nothing.&lt;/strong&gt; That's why &lt;code&gt;popstate&lt;/code&gt; isn't on by default and why &lt;code&gt;refresh()&lt;/code&gt; doesn't quietly ignore a call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check that the fix works before writing it down.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; advice is the example.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fix a mismatch between a name and its behavior in the design, not in the docs.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;refresh({ force: true })&lt;/code&gt; is the example.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When adding features, I want to keep checking that I'm not breaking what existing users already expect from the library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next time, I plan to write about how I worked on this with AI, including how design discussion and code review were split up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;npm: &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@standard-search-params/react" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.npmjs.com/package/@standard-search-params/react&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/otiswanglei/standard-search-params" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/otiswanglei/standard-search-params&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Japanese version (日本語版) is available on Zenn: &lt;a href="https://zenn.dev/wdp/articles/ece2138453fb9a" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://zenn.dev/wdp/articles/ece2138453fb9a&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is one of the most important programming languages in modern &lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;web development&lt;/a&gt;. It enables developers to create interactive, dynamic, and responsive websites. While HTML provides the structure of a webpage and CSS controls its appearance, JavaScript adds functionality and interactivity. From interactive forms and animations to complex web applications, JavaScript plays a vital role in enhancing the user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating Interactive Websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript makes websites interactive by responding to user actions such as clicks, scrolling, typing, and form submissions. Developers can use it to create dropdown menus, image sliders, pop-ups, and interactive navigation elements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic Content Updates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript allows developers to update webpage content without reloading the entire page. For example, an e-commerce website can dynamically display product filters, shopping cart updates, and search suggestions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Form Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript helps validate user input before submitting forms. It can check email formats, required fields, password strength, and other conditions, improving usability and reducing unnecessary server requests. Server-side validation is still essential for security.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Front-End Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript powers modern front-end libraries and frameworks such as React, Angular, and Vue.js. These tools help developers build reusable components, manage application data, and create sophisticated user interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Back-End Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With technologies such as Node.js, JavaScript can also run on servers. Developers can use it to build APIs, process requests, manage authentication, and communicate with databases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Asynchronous Operations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript supports asynchronous programming through Promises and async/await. These features allow applications to fetch data from APIs and perform background operations without unnecessarily blocking the user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Romanian CNP validator checks five things: 13 digits, a first digit from 1 to 9, a birth date that exists and is not in the future, a county code (01 to 48, 51, 52, or 70, which the new civil status system has used since May 2024) and the check digit. The check digit is the sum of the first 12 digits multiplied by the matching digits of 279146358279, mod 11, with a remainder of 10 written as 1. Store a valid CNP encrypted, with a keyed hash for lookups, because a plain SHA-256 of one can be brute forced in about half an hour on one CPU core.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your checkout, payroll or rental contract form asks for a Romanian personal numeric code (CNP), and a 13-digit regex accepts every typo. A Romanian CNP validator has to check more than the length: a digit for sex and century, a birth date that exists, a county code and a weighted check digit. This tutorial writes that check in TypeScript, decodes the parts, turns it into a zod rule and a React field that validates as you type, and ends with what to do before you store a single CNP. The field is &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/masked-input" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Masked Input&lt;/a&gt; from Wingo UI, the library we build, so we are not neutral. The validator is plain TypeScript you can paste anywhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What is the cod numeric personal format?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thirteen digits in seven parts, written S AA LL ZZ JJ NNN C. The &lt;a href="https://ro.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cod_numeric_personal_(Rom%C3%A2nia)" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Romanian Wikipedia article on the CNP&lt;/a&gt; documents each one. Here they are on &lt;code&gt;1960523123457&lt;/code&gt;, the sample code from the demo further down:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Digits&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Part&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;In the sample&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;S&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sex and century of birth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1: male, born 1900 to 1999&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2 to 3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AA&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Year of birth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;96&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4 to 5&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;LL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Month of birth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;05&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6 to 7&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ZZ&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Day of birth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;23&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8 to 9&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;JJ&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;County code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12: Cluj&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10 to 12&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NNN&lt;/td&gt;
